      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Yves Klein: Monochromes and Empty Space",
      "links": [],
      "level": 2
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Monochrome Propositions (1949–1957)",
      "links": [],
      "level": 4
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Yves Klein took up monochrome painting at the end\nof 1949. At the time he described this activity as \"a means\nof painting that is against painting, against all the anxieties\nof life, against everything\" [Stich 1994, pp. 23/253].\n\n\n\nAleksandr Rodchenko's earlier monochromes demarcate the point where\nart ends, and direct our attention to the uncharted territory \"beyond\nart\". Yves Klein re-invents the monochrome as an icon for the\nend of art, but for him the end of art is not a line which is to\nbe crossed. His perspective is strictly artistic: the end of art\nis the ultimate stage of art history; it is a space rather than\na point. Klein embraces the painting of monochromes as a way to\npractice art during the end of art. This paradoxical activity has\na mystic charge: transcending art = transcending everything = transcending\nthe vicissitudes of life.",
      "links": []
    },
